  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAnother hot and humid day is underway this afternoon amid strong\nridging parked over the eastern half of the country. At the time\nof writing, hourly temperatures at nearby ASOS sites were\nteetering around the upper 80s to low 90s. Coupled with dew\npoints in the 60s/70s, this has contributed to heat index \nvalues once again rising above the triple digit mark this\nafternoon. As we remain entrenched within this multi-day heat \nwave, Extreme Heat Warnings and Heat Advisories for the lowlands\nand foothills triumphs across the Central Appalachians. \n\nMuch of the same is expected in regards to the heat and humidity\non Friday, but upper level pressure falls amid the start of the\nbreakdown of the ridge will begin to take shape. Convective\ncomplexes that have thrived across the Upper Midwest over the\npast week may begin to encroach the lower Ohio Valley this\nweekend, bolstering increasing cloud coverage and probability of\nshowers and storms. \n\nThis leads to some uncertainty on whether heat headlines will \nspill into the Fourth of July. In the event the area receives an\nuptick in showers and storms, daytime temperatures and \nresulting heat indices may be spoiled by evaporative cooling \ncaused by downbursts accompanying any stronger storm. Opted\nalongside neighboring offices to allow at least one more\nforecast model cycle to showcase cloud and convective trends\nbefore making any decisions on heat for the holiday weekend. As\nit currently stands, heat headlines remain in place through\nFriday evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe breakdown of the upper level ridge will set the stage for a\ngradual return of active weather this weekend into next week.\nShortwave energy rounding the upper periphery of the ridge this\npast week will slither down into the Great Lakes region as the\ncenter of the 500mb ridge flattens, promoting diurnally driven \nshowers and storms increasing in coverage each day this weekend.\nLatest guidance suggests the alignment of storms will remain\ndraped across northern Ohio and Pennsylvania on Friday,\npotentially aiming for our far northwestern zones late in the\nevening. However, the loss of daytime heating around that time will\nstymie further encroachment of activity overnight Friday into\nSaturday. \n\nBetter upper level support slides down into the Central\nAppalachians in time for Independence Day, with scattered\nchances of showers and storms possible amid ongoing heat and\nhumidity. The Storm Prediction Center broad-brushes much of the\nnortheast United States under a risk of strong to severe\nthunderstorms for this weekend due to the expansive amounts of\ninstability and moisture festering overhead. \n\nThermal profiles depict a shift in flow pivoting out of the\nsouthwest by the end of the holiday weekend. This sets the stage\nfor warm, tropical moisture to usher into the forecast area and\npromote daily chances of isolated to scattered showers and\nstorms. As convective temperatures are breached each day within\nthis regime, showers and storms will fire up for the afternoon\nand evening hours, then gradually deteriorating with the loss of\ndaytime heating after sunset. Weak steering flow aloft suggests\nstorms that do develop will oftentimes be slow moving, and could\nlead to localized high water concerns, especially in the event\nof repetitive and training activity.",
